{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed168776016/4/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b71a7747\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nExtract the hidden two-color image by compacting the rows and columns of its complete mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid contains one pair of colors whose pixels occupy every cell in the\ncross product of their active rows and active columns; all other colors form\nthe surrounding filler or separators.\nRule\nFind that unique color pair, keep exactly the rows and columns touched by\nthe pair, and return the induced subgrid.\nKey insight\nThe target image is not chosen by color id; it is the only two-color mask\nwhose occupied pixels form a full rectangular row/column product after\ndeleting unused rows and columns.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dd6b8c4b\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nMove the closest reachable maroon pixels into the 3×3 block around the unique red center.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = orange floor, magenta walls, scattered maroon (9) pixels.\n- A unique red (2) pixel marks the center of a 3×3 destination block.\n\nRule:\n- Maroon pixels connected to the destination through floor/maroon cells (not blocked by walls) are candidates.\n- Rank candidates by distance to the destination; the closest ones fill the block in row-major order.\n- Moved maroons are erased (replaced with floor color).\n\nKey insight: Wall barriers restrict connectivity — only maroon pixels reachable via flood-fill through the floor qualify, so proximity alone isn't enough.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"689c358e\" lines=\"72-88\">\n<description>\nMark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.\n- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.\n- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.\n- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.\n- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1190bc91\" lines=\"36-49\">\n<description>\nComplete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.\n\nInput structure\n- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.\n- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.\n\nRule\n- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.\n- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.\n\nKey insight\n- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"182e5d0f\" lines=\"89-103\">\n<description>\nMove each gray marker along its green path until it reaches the cell next to a black-green-black gate, erasing the path behind it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- Foreground components are made from a path color, a paired gate-end color, and single marker pixels.\n- A gate is a straight end-center-end motif whose center has the path color and whose two ends share the gate-end color.\n\nRule:\n- In every 4-connected foreground component with exactly one marker and one gate, erase the marker and all path-color pixels except the gate center.\n- Place the marker on the unique path-color pixel adjacent to the gate center but not between the two gate ends.\n- Components without both a marker and a gate stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The black-green-black motif marks the stopping gate; the gray marker consumes the connected green path until it rests on the path cell immediately beside that gate.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"65b59efc\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nDecode a stamp map: read templates, markers, and palette from a tiled grid, then render stamps.\n\nInput structure:\n- Grid divided into K tile columns (each N wide) separated by 1-col dashed separators.\n- Top band (rows 0..N-1): K template masks, one per tile column.\n- Middle band (rows N+1..2N): marker pixels whose color picks a template.\n- Bottom band (rows 2N+2..): K palette swatches giving the output color per template.\n- Separator rows at rows N and 2N+1 (dashed pattern of sep color).\n\nRule:\n- Each marker's local position (lr, lc) within its tile column determines which N×N output block to stamp.\n- The marker's color identifies which template to use; the palette recolors the template.\n\nKey insight: Marker color encodes template index; local position within tile column encodes stamp block.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"470c91de\" lines=\"48-65\">\n<description>\nMove each teal-cornered filled rectangle one cell diagonally toward its teal corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- Dominant background surrounds several filled rectangular colored objects.\n- Each object has exactly one teal control pixel replacing one corner cell of\n  its rectangle.\n\nRule:\n- Treat the teal pixel as the missing corner of its colored rectangle.\n- Erase each original rectangle footprint, including the teal pixel.\n- Repaint the completed rectangle shifted one cell diagonally in the direction\n  of that teal corner.\n\nKey insight:\n- The control pixel's corner position gives both the missing cell to restore\n  and the diagonal motion vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2f767503\" lines=\"66-81\">\n<description>\nErase each target-color object crossed by the ray pointed by the bar-and-control marker.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There is one three-pixel straight marker bar and one singleton control pixel adjacent to the bar center.\n- The remaining non-background color forms multiple 4-connected target objects.\n\nRule:\n- Cast a straight ray from the marker bar center in the direction away from the adjacent control pixel.\n- Find every 4-connected target-color object that intersects that ray.\n- Recolor all pixels of those hit target objects to the background; all other pixels stay unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The whole object is erased when the center ray touches any of its pixels; the full marker bar width is not swept.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6e453dd6\" lines=\"39-52\">\n<description>\nSlide black shapes rightward to a grey separator, then shoot red rays from enclosed holes.\n\nInput structure:\n- A vertical grey (5) separator column divides the grid into left and right halves.\n- Black shapes sit on the left side against a background color (bg).\n\nRule:\n- Translate each black connected component rightward until its right edge is flush with the separator.\n- After sliding, find enclosed bg regions (holes) within the black shapes that touch the column just left of the separator.\n- For each row spanned by such a hole, emit a red ray across the entire right half of the grid.\n\nKey insight: Only holes adjacent to the separator trigger rays — the sliding creates enclosures that act as \"nozzles\" projecting rightward.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7ec998c9\" lines=\"14-28\">\n<description>\nDraw a blue folded corner-to-corner route through the lone marker's column.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
